264
00:10:16,130 --> 00:10:18,635
your kernel to hold a
pointer to that memory.

265
00:10:18,635 --> 00:10:20,255
You can malloc the memory

266
00:10:20,255 --> 00:10:26,180
and change the private data
to point to that memory,

267
00:10:26,180 --> 00:10:27,830
and then you'll be
able to access it for

268
00:10:27,830 --> 00:10:31,190
every function that passes
the file parameter,

269
00:10:31,190 --> 00:10:32,540
you just have to
remember to free it

270
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:34,950
when you do the release call.

271
00:10:35,170 --> 00:10:38,855
Going back to chart
device registration,

272
00:10:38,855 --> 00:10:39,920
we showed one example,

273
00:10:39,920 --> 00:10:41,690
actually we showed a
couple of examples in

274
00:10:41,690 --> 00:10:44,375
the last video but
there's really a couple

275
00:10:44,375 --> 00:10:47,390
of methods that are used to

276
00:10:47,390 --> 00:10:50,690
register a device or a
chart device in this case.

277
00:10:50,690 --> 00:10:52,970
One is you could just
have the kernel allocated

