00:12:46,360 --> 00:12:49,660
the character device
structure that we registered.

321
00:12:49,660 --> 00:12:52,315
How did we get from an

322
00:12:52,315 --> 00:12:55,030
open that takes a struct
inode and the struct file,

323
00:12:55,030 --> 00:12:57,100
how do we get the
c_dev pointer that we

324
00:12:57,100 --> 00:13:00,280
originally registered
for a specific device?

325
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:02,560
This is a pretty common
thing that you do

326
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:04,300
with Linux Kernel
programming as well.

327
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:06,370
You end up doing a
little bit of a where's

328
00:13:06,370 --> 00:13:09,010
Waldo type of following?

329
00:13:09,010 --> 00:13:10,810
Looking at the
details of whatever

330
00:13:10,810 --> 00:13:12,070
structure you're provided with

331
00:13:12,070 --> 00:13:14,410
a function call and figuring out

332
00:13:14,410 --> 00:13:17,200
how to get from there to
whatever structure you want,

333
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:19,855
when I look at the definition
of the inode structure,

334
00:13:19,855 --> 00:13:21,325
I'll see a union in there.

