372
00:14:45,925 --> 00:14:47,950
What this would be
if you wanted to use

373
00:14:47,950 --> 00:14:49,825
that to hold the private data,

374
00:14:49,825 --> 00:14:50,980
you could then use

375
00:14:50,980 --> 00:14:55,010
the scull_dev as a pointer
into private data.

376
00:14:57,090 --> 00:15:00,550
If you wanted to, you could
use it as private data,

377
00:15:00,550 --> 00:15:01,780
but you could also just use

378
00:15:01,780 --> 00:15:04,135
this to do general operations.

379
00:15:04,135 --> 00:15:06,100
If something in my open needed

380
00:15:06,100 --> 00:15:07,900
to access one of
these variables,

381
00:15:07,900 --> 00:15:09,190
I'd be able to just
do that through

382
00:15:09,190 --> 00:15:12,650
the local variable I
reference here in-depth.

383
00:15:14,430 --> 00:15:17,290
Another common thing that
we're going to see with

384
00:15:17,290 --> 00:15:20,199
Linux Kernel functions is
there's always a balance.

385
00:15:20,199 --> 00:15:22,675
Or a lot of times there's a
balance with things like,

386
00:15:22,675 --> 00:15:25,030
we've already seen this
within it and exit.

387
00:15:25,030 --> 00:15:27,775
Everything you do in it
you need to undo an exit.

388
00:15:27,775 --> 00:15:29,935
Releases the same way with open.

389
00:15:29,935 --> 00:15:32,245
Everything you
allocate and open,

390
00:15:32,245 --> 00:15:35,890
you have to make sure you
release in the release method.

391
00:15:35,890 --> 00:15:38,560
Really release is what you do

392
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:40,675
to deallocate
anything that was up.

393
00:15:40,675 --> 00:15:41,920
If you use, for instance,

394
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:44,110
the file pointer private
data and you set

395
00:15:44,110 --> 00:15:47,485
up some private database
on allocated memory,

396
00:15:47,485 --> 00:15:48,460
you'd want to make sure you're

397
00:15:48,460 --> 00:15:51,020
deallocating that and release.

398
00:15:51,210 --> 00:15:54,970
But the scull implementation,

399
00:15:54,970 --> 00:15:56,140
there's actually nothing that

400
00:15:56,140 --> 00:15:57,835
needs to happen in release,

401
00:15:57,835 --> 00:16:00,100
you'll see, you'll see
an empty function here.

402
00:16:00,100 --> 00:16:03,670
The reason is just because
the data that was pointed to

403
00:16:03,670 --> 00:16:05,500
in the file PE private
data structure

404
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:07,240
was allocated init_module.

405
00:16:07,240 --> 00:16:11,740
You'll notice that it was
referencing the scull device,

406
00:16:11,740 --> 00:16:14,770
but that was actually
allocated here in init_module,

407
00:16:14,770 --> 00:16:16,975
so that'll be freed
and in module exit.

408
00:16:16,975 --> 00:16:18,580
Again, you have to look at where

409
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:22,480
your memory allocation
is and take whatever is

410
00:16:22,480 --> 00:16:26,395
basically the opposite of
that implementation and

411
00:16:26,395 --> 00:16:28,360
put your release memory or

412
00:16:28,360 --> 00:16:30,865
you're free of
memory in that case.

413
00:16:30,865 --> 00:16:33,040
If we need to
deallocate anything in

414
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:35,170
open that was allocated,

415
00:16:35,170 --> 00:16:37,150
what would happen if the
User space programmer

416
00:16:37,150 --> 00:16:38,875
forgets to close the file?

417
00:16:38,875 --> 00:16:41,050
It turns out the Kernel
is going to clean this up

418
00:16:41,050 --> 00:16:43,405
